module gunbc.roster_registry

data roster_registry_taxonomy_note: String = "Group-of-units standard representation (operator ruling 2026-07-22, roadmap group 5a): a plurality of units must have a standard representation, never a nickname. Membership in a group is known exactly one of four ways — POSITIONAL (containment: being-in IS being-there, e.g. a module's members or a directory-grain lane), DERIVED (a computed relation: closure reachability, inhabitance, measurement), FRONTIER (asserted per-row WITH a reason and a dissolve_on trigger — countable, prioritizable, the typed self-host-frontier pattern), or NICKNAME (asserted, string-keyed, no trigger — the violation this registry exists to make visible). The migration pipeline for any hand-kept list is mint -> frontier -> query: a new group lands as typed frontier rows, and graduates to a derived query when its membership predicate is modelable. Every known unit-naming roster is enrolled below; the lens v2.lens.roster_registry holds each registration to ByContainment, ByDerivation, or DeclaredFrontier-with-nonempty-reason-and-trigger."

data roster_registry_enforcement_intent_convergence_note: String = "Convergence, not implementation: the enforcement-intent thread (DESIGN open thread 'enforcement intent — ask once, compile forever'; docs/plans/enforcement-intent-design.md) absorbs this registry when StandingIntent / LensContract land — its subject_roster IS this concept (a registered group of units with declared membership provenance). Do not build that machinery here; this carrier stays the minimal data authority and the lens stays a data-shape check until that thread lands and this registry becomes its subject_roster carrier."

data roster_registry_visibility_note: String = "Honesty bound: the lens sees only ENROLLED registrations. An unregistered bare list elsewhere in the tree is invisible to it — until enforcement-intent lands, unregistered nickname lists are caught at review by the group-5a rule (registry admission is the review bar for any new 'pool'/'class'/'lane'/'family' String key), not by this lens. The one residue this note formerly walled — NON_FOLD_RESIDUE_ROSTER, a host-side Rust const whose per-row reasons and triggers lived as comments — is re-homed: the rows are gunbc.non_fold_residue.non_fold_residue_frontier (typed FrontierRow, host census reads through non_fold_residue_units_from_module_source in cli_run.rs) and the roster is enrolled below. RESIDUE ACCOUNTING (corrected 2026-07-25): this note formerly claimed 'no known unregistered residue remains' and that claim was FALSE — a CI-land census that day found five unenrolled hand lists outside gunbc.ci_layer_roots, whose own enrollment lane was complete and had made the gap look closed: ci_floor_required_artifact_names and gunbc_ci_floor_batch_wall_budget_seconds (gunbc.ci_spec), commit_gate_roster (gunbc.commit_workflow), gunbc_ci_cheap_floor_gate_membership (v2.workflow.ci_floor_plan), and ci_cost_floor_exempt_step_names (gunbc.ci_materialization). All five are enrolled below. The lesson is this note's own visibility bound applied to itself: enrollment completeness in one module is not evidence of completeness in the tree, so a 'no residue remains' claim is only ever as good as its last census and carries the census date. CORRECTION (2026-07-25, same day): the paragraph above previously named GENERATED_STAGE0_FILES as residue that 'cannot carry a RosterRegistration until its rows re-home into .dag'. That was WRONG, and it is a second instance of this note's own lesson — a residue claim asserted from one side of the tree without checking the other. The rows were ALREADY in .dag as gunbc.stage0_emit_model.generated_stage0_files, and the Rust const's own header already names that decl 'Registry authority'. So the defect was never a missing re-home; it was a live FORK of two hand copies, which had silently drifted 98 (.dag) versus 101 (Rust) — the .dag side missing three real generated files. The drift is repaired and the roster is enrolled below; the fork itself closes only when regen_stage0 reads the .dag rows and the const is deleted. The lesson generalizes: 'this cannot be enrolled yet' is a claim about the tree and must be verified against the tree, exactly like the 'no residue remains' claim it sits beside. By contrast regen_input_sources (cli_run.rs) is NOT residue and is deliberately not listed: it derives its set by walking the import closure from the entry roots, so it is already ByDerivation in substance. GROWTH BASIS, and how to re-derive it: the point of enrolling a hand list is that its ACCUMULATION becomes observable, so the question a future reader asks is 'has this roster grown since it was registered, and what grew it'. That is answered from git rather than from a count stored here — a stored count is the hand-count-beside-structure class this registry exists to make visible, and it would rot silently (the GENERATED_STAGE0_FILES figure above sat at 54 in prior notes while the const had reached 101). TWO TRAPS make the re-derivation non-obvious. First, working clones here are SHALLOW (a default worktree carries ~12 commits), so git log over a roster's history returns almost nothing and looks like 'this list has never changed' — run git fetch --depth=400 origin main first, and treat any basis older than that horizon as truncated rather than absent. Second, line numbers move, so trace the DECLARATION not a line range: git log -L then the decl's current start,end line range, and for a true growth series count the roster's elements at each touching commit (git show COMMIT:FILE piped through a count of the row marker) rather than trusting the touch count, since most commits edit a row without adding one. Basis (2026-07-25, post-merge of #7199): ci_floor_required_artifact_names 18 entries; witness_exclusion_frontier 74 rows; bin_witness_wet_entries 53; commit_gate_roster 44; known_red_probe_entries 7; gunbc_ci_floor_batch_wall_budget_seconds 7; gunbc_ci_cheap_floor_gate_membership 3; ci_cost_floor_exempt_step_names 3. These are a DATED SNAPSHOT for comparison, not an authority: re-derive before relying on any of them. THE SNAPSHOT'S OWN STALENESS IS THE RECEIPT for why this note stores a recipe instead of a count column. The basis was first taken at main 4aed680509 and read: bin roster 17, exclusion frontier 73, bin_witness_wet 51, commit_gate_roster 45. Merging main a few hours later (bringing #7199) moved FOUR of those seven rows in ONE merge — 17 -> 18 (the bin roster gaining resolution_divergence_census, i.e. the very row this registry names as the expected first faller grew again while being registered), 73 -> 74, 51 -> 53, and commit_gate_roster 45 -> 44, which SHRANK. So a stored count here would have been wrong before the PR that wrote it even landed, and the shrink shows the drift is not monotone, which is why 'has it grown' must be answered by re-derivation over git rather than by comparing against a number someone typed. The rate visible in that one merge — four of seven rosters, inside a single day — is itself the finding this registry exists to surface. CLOSING CORRECTION (2026-08-20): the GENERATED_STAGE0_FILES thread above ends, and not the way its own last sentence predicted. That sentence said the fork closes when regen_stage0 reads the .dag rows and the Rust const is deleted. regen_stage0 was deleted at the root instead (#8406, the regen cut), which took the roster PRODUCER with it and left the .dag rows hand-maintained by nobody — so the fork did not close, it went quiet, and #8587 had to hand-edit the producerless roster to remove two files the emit had stopped producing. Both hand copies are now gone: the .dag roster is deleted, and the surviving Rust const is HAND_MAINTAINED_STAGE0_FILES, which is not the same fact (it is the crate-layout claim, generated from v2.compiler.self_host.stage0_crate_layout by a wet actuator). The generated population is derived as the complement of that claim over the observed tree — gunbc.stage0_rust_source_lifecycle_scaffold derived_generated_stage0_repo_paths — so there is one authority and no roster to register. The general lesson for this note is the one it keeps re-learning from a new angle: a dissolution trigger names the future someone expected, and deleting the trigger's SUBJECT satisfies nothing while making the row unfalsifiable."

data roster_registry_upcoming_targets_note: String = "Walls for in-flight lanes (registry admission is the rule): (a) the bulk-native flip's 744 frontier rows MUST land ByDerivation — the native_routing_frontier registration below is the template (subject-keyed, closure-derived membership, equivalence receipt); (b) chore-remedy classification is already by-construction (subject types) and needs no registration; (c) the deploy lease/generation guard reuses Grant/containment-prefix plus git ancestry — no new group concept, nothing to enroll; (d) machine-shape scheduling and CI-ergonomics lanes: any 'pool'/'class'/'lane' String key at review enrolls here or is rejected as a nickname; (e) the long/ dir lane was BLESSED Form-C on the ground that storage grain came WITH a construction backstop — the five-second eval deadline making an over-budget fast-lane witness unwritable-in-place. THAT BLESSING IS RETRACTED (2026-08-04): the deadline is evaluated only while a witness RUNS, and moving a file under the long/ prefix is exactly the act that stops it running, so the backstop is disarmed by the move it was supposed to govern. Measured cost of the wrong blessing: gunbc#7762 commit 0cfd199f8 relocated a thirteen-function discriminating matrix into test/claim/long/ under the title fix CI fast-lane budget failures, adding a claim_batch command line as a data row and no cadence — zero executed coverage, admission holding, and a later commit on the same branch split eight of the thirteen back only because a reviewer looked. The lane keeps its ByContainment registration below because the containment IS still how the files are organized, but containment no longer decides admission: gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze enumerates the frozen legacy identities and anything outside it refuses (std.witness_admission UnclassifiedPathDeferral). The general lesson for a future Form-C blessing is that a backstop must be checked against the transformation it is claimed to prevent, not against the state it describes."

type GroupMembership
  = ByContainment
  | ByDerivation { predicate: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef }
  | DeclaredFrontier { reason: String, dissolution: std.dissolution.DissolutionCondition }

type RosterRegistration {
  roster: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef
  unit_kind: String
  membership: GroupMembership
}

fn roster_decl(module_path: String, decl_name: String) -> std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
  std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
    module_path: module_path,
    decl_name: decl_name,
    field: std.decl_ref.WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data roster_registry: List<RosterRegistration> = [
  RosterRegistration {
    roster: roster_decl(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "unresolved_method_frontier"),
    unit_kind: "method call whose receiver establishes no method surface, keyed (module, method, receiver_shape)",
    membership: DeclaredFrontier {
      reason: "The method-existence wall refuses an unresolved method rather than inheriting the receiver type, but it can only PROVE absence against a receiver that has a complete declared surface. These rows are the calls whose receiver has none — each an upstream receiver-resolution defect (a lambda parameter whose type never propagates, a coproduct payload typed as its variant name, a bare type variable, an optional whose cardinality is dropped before lookup) rather than a fact about the method. Refusing them would fabricate a refusal over correct working code, including the v2 tokenizer's own LexMatchThunk idiom; admitting them silently would fabricate a success. The rows carry the residue as counted MethodExistenceFrontierAdmitted diagnostics instead. The key includes the receiver SHAPE because that shape IS the evidence, so a new call is refused unless it reproduces the exact unestablished shape the row was measured on.",
      dissolution: std.dissolution.unbound_dissolution(description: "feature:primitive-realization-single-authority — one PrimitiveDefinition identity joining semantic definition, interpreter dispatch and per-target emit handler, at which point every receiver has a complete declared surface and the wall decides these directly. Each row also dissolves individually when its own named receiver-resolution defect is fixed.")
    }
  },
  RosterRegistration {
    roster: roster_decl(module_path: "gunbc.ci_layer_roots", decl_name: "witness_exclusion_frontier"),
