    unit_kind: "workflow step name (cost-floor exemption)",
    membership: DeclaredFrontier {
      reason: "steps exempted from the materialization cost floor, keyed BY STEP NAME and resolved to script identities against the live workflow at gate time rather than held as a stale copied string; each named step is a sub-second environment write whose store-tier discharge would cost more than the recompute it would save, so the exemption is a cost fact asserted per row rather than measured",
      dissolution: std.dissolution.unbound_dissolution(description: "per-step cost attribution lands so exemption derives from measured sub-threshold cost rather than a named list; per row, a row naming a step the live workflow no longer contains deletes (the gate-time resolution already makes such a row loud rather than silent)")
    }
  },
  RosterRegistration {
    roster: roster_decl(module_path: "gunbc.ci_spec", decl_name: "gunbc_ci_floor_batch_wall_budget_note"),
    unit_kind: "dated budget-raise receipt (prose-embedded)",
    membership: DeclaredFrontier {
      reason: "CENSUS-CLASS DEBT, registered not rewritten (census 2026-07-25): the operator-signed raise discipline requires appending a dated receipt naming the run id and the enrollment that grew the batch, so the note has accreted a genuine SEQUENCE OF CHANGE EVENTS — batch 3 alone reads 1320 -> 1440 -> 1680 -> 2100 across three signed raises — held as prose inside a 10KB String literal beside the seven-int list it documents. Because the receipts are narrated rather than rowed, the discipline is enforced by author diligence instead of construction: nothing can count the raises, order them, attribute one to its enrollment, or gate a raise that lands without its receipt, and the note's own text records the tell (one raise's ENROLLMENT THAT GREW THE BATCH field reads 'none, and that mismatch with this discipline's template IS the receipt')",
      dissolution: std.dissolution.unbound_dissolution(description: "raise receipts become typed rows (date, run id, batch identity, prior value, new value, signer, attributed cause) so the signed-raise discipline is checkable by construction rather than narrated; rides the carrier's own follow-up (b) identity-keyed budgets on the ComputationIdentity lane, and retires entirely when #7216's derived clamps replace the literal ceilings with a re-signed rate")
    }
  },
  RosterRegistration {
    roster: roster_decl(module_path: "gunbc.ci_workflow", decl_name: "gunbc_ci_floor_step_timeout_discovery_flip_note"),
    unit_kind: "dated step-timeout bounce receipt (prose-embedded)",
    membership: DeclaredFrontier {
      reason: "CENSUS-CLASS DEBT, registered not rewritten (census 2026-07-25): the same shape as the batch-budget note above, one layer up — a 6KB String whose body is the step cap's bounce history (30 -> 60 -> 90 -> 120 -> 180 -> 270 -> 55), each bounce citing a run id and a date, none of them a queryable row. The note itself names why this matters ('the record of the budget being raised to fit'), so the sequence is load-bearing evidence about a recurring cost regression, yet answering 'how many times has this cap moved, and which enrollment moved it' requires reading the prose end to end. Excluded from this class after inspection: ci_floor_resolve_receipt_note and known_red_frontier_note are comparably large and dated but record MEASUREMENTS and per-entry diagnoses, not a sequence of budget mutations, so they are not receipt ledgers and are deliberately not enrolled here",
      dissolution: std.dissolution.unbound_dissolution(description: "bounce events become typed receipt rows alongside the batch-budget receipts above (one shared receipt shape, not a second ledger); the prose history retires with the step cap itself when per-batch wall budgets carry typed receipts and the cap stops being the diagnostic")
    }
  },
  RosterRegistration {
    roster: roster_decl(module_path: "v2.workflow.rust_crate_partition", decl_name: "stage0_partition_crate_rows"),
    unit_kind: "stage0 seed module basename per partition crate",
    membership: DeclaredFrontier {
      reason: "the hand-drawn stage0 crate split: per-crate module membership is hand-listed (stage0_std_primitive_modules and siblings) while the true relation is the module import closure — enrolled 2026-07-23 off two drift incidents this class caused silently (std_currency absent from std-core after #6977 added the measure→currency edge, so v1-stage0-std-core stopped building standalone; the partition allow-block lacked the monolith's recursion_limit so v1-stage0-extdeps-languages failed on serde json_internal). Nothing in CI builds the partition crate libs, so the drift had zero signal (sheet row ts-group-partition-drift)",
      dissolution: std.dissolution.unbound_dissolution(description: "the emitted-crate-partition thread derives the crate layout ByDerivation from the module graph (docs/plans/emitted-crate-partition-design.md), at which point the hand lists delete; interim backstop = an executing per-crate cargo-check consumer in the CI build job")
    }
  },
  RosterRegistration {
    roster: roster_decl(module_path: "gunbc.materialization_provider_targets", decl_name: "materialization_provider_targets"),
    unit_kind: "ad-hoc cache consumer target (CacheInterfaceId) of the std.materialization_provider contract",
    membership: DeclaredFrontier {
      reason: "each row names an existing cited cache row that today answers lookups through an ad-hoc carrier and later consumes the contract's typed provider_serve/provider_admit outcomes; per-row reason and dissolve_on live in the ProviderContractTarget rows (parent mandate 6B, model-only — targets declared, no consumer flips)",
      dissolution: std.dissolution.unbound_dissolution(description: "consumer flips route each cache's lookups through std.materialization_provider; rows retire per flip and the roster deletes when empty")
